# Bratislava new-build prices are standing still, and the gap between dispositions is wider than the market's own movement

{cqNameEn} of {cqYear} on the Bratislava new-build market means stable prices, supply without significant change and sales in line with the preceding months. On a comparable sample of projects the average price {panelM2DirEn} by {panelM2PctEn}% over five months, from {panelM2From} to {panelM2To} €/m² incl. VAT. Supply on the same sample {panelSupplyDirEn} by {panelSupplyPctEn}%. Sales reached {cqSales} flats for the quarter.

As of {asOfEn} the supply on the new-build market consisted, according to Residata's data, of {cqSupply} flats across {cqProjects} projects. On the fixed sample of {panelProjects} projects we have tracked since {panelFromEn}, supply {panelSupplyDirEn} from {panelSupplyFrom} to {panelSupplyTo} flats, or {panelSupplyPctEn}%. Sales on the same sample run at {panelMonthlySales} flats a month on average.

## The average price says less than the disposition

The average price of a flat on offer is {cqM2} €/m² incl. VAT, but practically no disposition costs that. One-room flats cost {r1M2} €/m² incl. VAT and three-room flats {r3M2} €/m² incl. VAT — a gap of {roomSpreadPctEn}%, wider than any movement of the market in recent months. The curve is not rising but U-shaped: the dearest metre is in small and large flats, and the cheapest in the two- and three-room middle.

In absolute price, which is what a mortgage is granted against, the market looks different. A one-room flat costs {r1Price} € on average at {r1AreaEn} m², a two-room one {r2Price} € at {r2AreaEn} m², a three-room one {r3Price} € at {r3AreaEn} m², and a four-room or larger {r4Price} € at {r4AreaEn} m².

