## The development of supply and sales on the Bratislava new-build market

{cqSales} flats were sold over the quarter. Most of them were two-room ({r2Sold}), followed by three-room ({r3Sold}), one-room ({r1Sold}) and four-room or larger ({r4Sold}). Sales were not distorted by an exceptionally high figure in one of the large projects — the strongest of them accounted for {qTopSellerN} flats.

The average achieved price of sold flats reached {cqSoldM2} €/m² incl. VAT, practically matching the average asking price of {cqM2} €/m² incl. VAT. Behind that is above all increased demand in {qD1} and {qD2}, which are dearer than the average — in {qD1} sales rose from {qD1Then} to {qD1Now} flats and in {qD2} from {qD2Then} to {qD2Now}.

A standing average does not mean developers leave prices alone. Over the quarter the price of {rpUnits} flats changed across {rpProjects} projects, {rpUpEn}% of the changes upward at a median of {rpStepEn}%; it touched only {rpShareEn}% of the offer, however, so it shifts the average by about {rpEffectEn}%. Working against it is new supply — {qNewUnits} flats arrived at an average of {qNewMeanM2} €/m² incl. VAT, {newBelowPctEn}% below the market, and the two forces very nearly cancel.

## A view on where the Bratislava new-build market is heading

Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata: "The market is standing still in the most literal sense — supply and the price level have both moved by less than one per cent over five months. That does not mean nothing is happening. Price lists do change; they are simply cancelled out by cheaper new supply.

What matters more to a buyer is that the city average tells them nothing about what they will pay. The gap between a one-room and a three-room flat is {roomSpreadPctEn}% per metre, many times the movement of the market. Comparing offers through a city average means comparing something that exists in no price list.

Unless interest rates and household purchasing power change materially, we expect this state to continue. What we will be watching above all is whether new supply keeps being launched below the level of existing price lists — that is the point at which the average would start to fall."

**Author:** Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata · tomas@residata.eu
